Primitive counterexample filter
  If no speed is divisible by k+1, then t=1/(k+1) is immediately lonely.
  Therefore every counterexample must contain a speed divisible by k+1.
  Small mod-filter scans are possible with scan_mod_filter(), but are
  omitted from the default run because endpoint protection is quadratic
  in endpoints and intervals for each speed set.

Takeaways
  1. Boundary-only tight examples have many unprotected endpoints;
     no scanned tight example is remotely close to all-protected.
  2. The trivial t=1/(k+1) witness is exactly the first filter:
     any counterexample must contain a speed divisible by k+1.
  3. Near-tight positive-gap examples can have high protected ratios,
     but still leave explicit unprotected endpoints/gaps.
  4. The next obstruction should be a protection-peeling lemma:
     full open covers would require an impossible all-protected core.
